"pct_control":null,"donor_test":"not significant","donor_padj":0.753277,"donor_log2fc":-0.125701,"donor_lfcse":0.0965921,"donor_log2fc_shrunk":-0.0065023}],"modules":[],"degas_bulk_de":false,"drug_target":false}},"description":[{"gene_id":"ENSG00000099953","entrez_id":["4320"],"uniprot":["P24347"],"ncbi_summary":"Proteins of the matrix metalloproteinase (MMP) family are involved in the breakdown of extracellular matrix in normal physiological processes, such as embryonic development, reproduction, and tissue remodeling, as well as in disease processes, such as arthritis and metastasis. Most MMP's are secreted as inactive proproteins which are activated when cleaved by extracellular proteinases. However, the enzyme encoded by this gene is activated intracellularly by furin within the constitutive secretory pathway. Also in contrast to other MMP's, this enzyme cleaves alpha 1-proteinase inhibitor but weakly degrades structural proteins of the extracellular matrix. [provided by RefSeq, Jul 2008].","ncbi_source":"NCBI Gene summary (RefSeq), via MyGene.info","uniprot_function":[{"accession":"P24347","text":"May play an important role in the progression of epithelial malignancies."}],"uniprot_source":"UniProtKB/Swiss-Prot Function, release 2026_04","retrieved":"2026-11-06"}],"structures":[],"alphafold_accessions":[]}